[Music]  we know that mitochondria may indeed be  endosymbiosis in the sense that millions  of years ago these tiny bacteria were  engulfed by larger cells and ultimately  evolved to be codependent with ourselves  we provide them with foodstuffs and  protection from predation and in turn  they provide us with the majority of the  ATP energy currency that our cells need  to do work well you may not realize  however is that each mitochondrion has  its own DNA its own genome so what that  means is that while we are accustomed to  thinking of ourselves and their genomes  in terms of what exists in the nucleus  all of the genes that we think of as the  ones we inherit from our mother and from  our father we cannot forget that the  mitochondria in every single cell also  carry their own DNA genomes now where  did those DNA genomes come from well  another interesting fact is that we only  get mitochondria from our mothers the  mitochondria come with the egg while the  sperm that we got from our fathers in  terms of fertilization do not contribute  any mitochondria to what will ultimately  develop in embryogenesis into us into an  adult human being so this is of course a  debt that we owe our mothers they are  the source of our mitochondria but then  if you want to think about what we  inherit and are there possible mutations  that we might inherit we can't just  think about what we inherit in terms of  the nuclear genomes what we hear it from  our Father and from our mother we also  must think about what mitochondria we  inherited from our mothers it was fairly  recently when we discovered that there  are a number of human diseases usually  metabolic diseases that are tied to the  inheritance of mutations in the  mitochondrial genome many of these  diseases as you might expect are related  to lower than normal  levels of ATP or defects for example in  the electron transport chain defects  that really affect if you will the  energy metabolism that we all need so  crucially for all of nice processes so  the question is where did these defects  come from in mitochondria and how can we  understand the process of inheritance  that occurs let's begin by thinking  about my cells mitochondria and what the  makeup of those mitochondria might look  like so if we have a cell from our  bodies we have a nucleus and then we may  have multiple mitochondria in a cell  usually we'll have anywhere from ten to  twenty nine Condry now when we think  about this we generally think of the  nuclei or a single nucleus in a single  cell as having a particular genome but  what we don't usually think about is  that the mitochondria in a cell are in  fact a population they are not all the  same and in particular if you think  about their genomes their genomes may  not be all the same so here we have  shown a number of mitochondria and we're  distinguishing their genomes by color so  we have let's say four mitochondria that  we are designated as having one  particular genome in purple we have two  in pink and we have three in orange now  let's suppose that there's a defect in  the mitochondrial genomes and the defect  is associated with these two  mitochondria that are pink what this  means is that the defects are associated  with two mitochondria but we have  another four in fact seven mitochondria  that are otherwise perfectly healthy and  so when it comes to the state of that  cell  whether or not there's a negative impact  of those mutations will depend on how  many mitochondria are defective  mitochondrial diseases are really quite  devastating if you think about it it's  not really surprising because given the  low that mitochondria play in providing  the majority of ATP that we need any  disease that disrupts their ability to  produce ATP to manufacture ATP can have  significant metabolic impacts so  mitochondrial disease often involves for  example muscle weakness and wasting  because muscles use a significant amount  of ATP  it also can involve cognitive defects  sort of degeneration of the brain  because on what as one would expect  neurons and nerve cells in the brain  also utilize an enormous amount of ATP  how common our mitochondria disease is  they're really one of the more common  kinds of genetic diseases for example in  the United States where you will see a  frequency of roughly one in five to ten  thousand births might have some kind of  mitochondrial disease because of the  severity or the possible severity of  mitochondrial disease you also  frequently see lethality by h2 so that  quite often if there's a severe  expression of a mitochondrial disease  you might have a stillborn kind of  situation or you may also have a case  where the child dies by age two so  clearly this is a very severe kind of  disease but it also reveals a remarkably  different kind of inheritance than  anything we've seen before  you  [Music]

